var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testing/#Testing-with-Test.jl","page":"Testing with Test.jl","text":"VeriJul testbenches are ordinary Julia code, so the standard Test library works with them. Inside @testbench tasks it also knows about simulated time:\n\n@test inside a testbench records into the @testset the testbench was launched from, so its results appear in the normal test summary.\nA failing @test says when in the simulation it failed: the simulation time, the cycle and the testbench.\nA testbench that throws fails with a TestbenchError that says the same.\n\nThis tutorial tests the GCD unit from Waiting for events.\n\nusing VeriJul, Test, Random\n\ndut = load_dut(joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"gcd.v\"), \"gcd\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nreset!(sim)","title":"Testing with Test.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testing/#@test-inside-testbenches","page":"Testing with Test.jl","text":"A driver testbench sends random pairs to the design, and a checker testbench compares every answer with Julia's own gcd. They talk through a Channel, so the checker knows which inputs each answer belongs to:\n\n@testbench function driver(sim, jobs, n)\n    for _ in 1:n\n        a, b = rand(1:500), rand(1:500)\n        put!(jobs, (a, b))\n        sim.dut.a = a\n        sim.dut.b = b\n        sim.dut.start = 1\n        @tick sim\n        sim.dut.start = 0\n        @wait_until sim sim.dut.done == 1 max_cycles=5000\n    end\n    close(jobs)\nend\n\n@testbench function checker(sim, jobs)\n    for (a, b) in jobs\n        wait_edge(sim, :done; max_cycles=5000)\n        @test sim.dut.result == gcd(a, b)\n    end\nend\n\n@testbench launchers pass extra arguments on to the function. Launch them inside a @testset and wait for them there:\n\nRandom.seed!(42)\njobs = Channel{Tuple{Int,Int}}(Inf)\n@testset \"gcd matches Base.gcd\" begin\n    d = launch_driver(sim, jobs, 50)\n    c = launch_checker(sim, jobs)\n    wait(d)\n    wait(c)\nend\nTest Summary:        | Pass  Total  Time\ngcd matches Base.gcd |   50     50  0.3s\n\nAll 50 checks are counted in the @testset, even though they ran in a separate task.","title":"@test inside testbenches"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testing/#What-a-failure-looks-like","page":"Testing with Test.jl","text":"When a check inside a testbench fails, Test prints the usual report with a Context line that tells you where to look in the waveform:\n\ngcd matches Base.gcd: Test Failed at test/runtests.jl:24\n  Expression: sim.dut.result == gcd(a, b)\n   Evaluated: 0x0004 == 5\n     Context: simulation time 180, cycle 19, testbench `checker`\n\nWith enable_trace! on, jump to time 180 in the VCD file.","title":"What a failure looks like"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testing/#A-testbench-that-throws","page":"Testing with Test.jl","text":"If a testbench throws, for example when a max_cycles limit runs out, wait and fetch throw a TaskFailedException that wraps a TestbenchError. Inside a @testset that counts as an error, and the message names the testbench and the simulation time. You can also look at the fields:\n\n@testbench function never_started(sim)\n    @tick sim                                       # let the last `done` pulse end\n    @wait_until sim sim.dut.done == 1 max_cycles=20\nend\n\nerr = try\n    wait(launch_never_started(sim))\ncatch e\n    e\nend\ntb = err.task.exception\n(tb.testbench, tb.time, tb.cycle, typeof(tb.error))\n(:never_started, 16190, 1620, WaitTimeout)\n\nThe message of the original error is kept:\n\nsprint(showerror, tb.error)\n\"WaitTimeout: `sim.dut.done == 1` did not happen within 20 cycles (gave up at time 16190, cycle 1620)\"\n\nA testbench that waits for a failed child testbench passes the child's TestbenchError on unchanged, so the report always points at the testbench where things went wrong.","title":"A testbench that throws"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testing/#Organizing-a-test-suite","page":"Testing with Test.jl","text":"A test/runtests.jl for a design usually builds the design once and runs a fresh simulator per test set:\n\nusing VeriJul, Test\n\nconst SRC = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"rtl\", \"gcd.v\")\n\nfunction with_sim(f)\n    dut = load_dut(SRC, \"gcd\")          # cached after the first build\n    sim = Simulator(dut)\n    start_clock!(sim)\n    reset!(sim)\n    try\n        f(sim)\n    finally\n        stop_clock!(sim)\n        destroy!(dut)\n    end\nend\n\n@testset \"gcd\" begin\n    @testset \"small numbers\" begin\n        with_sim() do sim\n            wait(launch_checker(sim, ...))\n        end\n    end\n    @testset \"random numbers\" begin\n        # ...\n    end\nend\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\nLaunch testbenches from inside the @testset they belong to. On Julia 1.10–1.12, a task started with plain @async doesn't know the surrounding test set, so testbenches launched from it record their results outside it.\nAlways wait (or fetch) the testbenches you launch. Otherwise an error in one of them goes unnoticed and the test set ends before they finish.\nA @testset inside a testbench groups its checks as usual: it is recorded into the test set the testbench was launched from, and its failures get the simulation context too.\nGive every wait a max_cycles (or timeout), so a stuck design fails the test instead of hanging it.\n\n\n\nNext: Streams and scoreboards.","title":"Organizing a test suite"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/waiting/#Waiting-for-events","page":"Waiting for events","text":"@tick waits a fixed number of clock cycles. That's enough for the filter, which always answers one cycle later, but many designs take as long as they take: a divider, a bus that inserts wait states, a state machine waiting for input. A testbench for them has to wait for something to happen, not for a number of cycles.\n\nThis tutorial uses examples/gcd.v, which computes the greatest common divisor of two numbers by repeated subtraction. You pulse start; busy is high while it works, and done pulses for one cycle when result is ready. How long that takes depends on the numbers.\n\nusing VeriJul\n\ndut = load_dut(joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"gcd.v\"), \"gcd\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nreset!(sim)\ndut\nDUT(gcd, 8 ports)\n  input   clk              1 bit\n  input   rst              1 bit\n  input   start            1 bit\n  output  busy             1 bit\n  output  done             1 bit\n  input   a                16 bits\n  input   b                16 bits\n  output  result           16 bits\n\nA small helper starts a computation:\n\nfunction start!(sim, a, b)\n    sim.dut.a = a\n    sim.dut.b = b\n    sim.dut.start = 1\n    @tick sim\n    sim.dut.start = 0\nend","title":"Waiting for events"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/waiting/#Waiting-for-a-condition","page":"Waiting for events","text":"@wait_until checks a condition now and after every rising edge, and returns once it is true. Its result is the number of edges it waited:\n\nstart!(sim, 48, 18)\ncycles = @wait_until sim dut.done == 1\n(cycles, dut.result)\n(9, 0x0006)\n\nDifferent inputs take a different number of cycles, which is why counting cycles with @tick wouldn't work here:\n\nstart!(sim, 1000, 3)\ncycles = @wait_until sim dut.done == 1\n(cycles, dut.result)\n(339, 0x0001)\n\nThe condition can be any Julia expression, for example @wait_until sim dut.busy == 0 && dut.result != 0. If it is already true, @wait_until returns 0 without waiting.","title":"Waiting for a condition"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/waiting/#Always-set-a-limit","page":"Waiting for events","text":"If the design never gets there, because of a bug or a missing start, an unbounded wait hangs the test. Pass max_cycles and a WaitTimeout is thrown instead. Its message quotes the condition and says when it gave up:\n\n@tick sim                                           # let the last `done` pulse end\ntry\n    @wait_until sim dut.done == 1 max_cycles=50     # nothing was started\ncatch err\n    sprint(showerror, err)\nend\n\"WaitTimeout: `dut.done == 1` did not happen within 50 cycles (gave up at time 4020, cycle 403)\"\n\ntimeout is also available, as a wall-clock limit in seconds, the same as for tick.","title":"Always set a limit"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/waiting/#Waiting-for-a-signal-edge","page":"Waiting for events","text":"wait_edge waits for a port to change. For 1-bit ports, edge is :rising (the default) or :falling; :change works for ports of any width. It returns the port's new value:\n\nstart!(sim, 12, 8)\nwait_edge(sim, :busy; edge=:falling, max_cycles=100)\n(dut.done, dut.result)\n(0x01, 0x0004)\n\nsim.cycle counts rising edges of the main clock, so it can measure how long something takes:\n\ndut.a = 255; dut.b = 15; dut.start = 1\nwait_edge(sim, :busy)                  # the design accepted the request\ndut.start = 0\nc0 = sim.cycle\nwait_edge(sim, :done; max_cycles=1000)\n(latency = sim.cycle - c0, result = dut.result)\n(latency = 19, result = 0x000f)\n\nwait_edge samples the port after every evaluation of the design, that is after each edge of any clock, which is when outputs change.","title":"Waiting for a signal edge"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/waiting/#The-function-form","page":"Waiting for events","text":"wait_until is the function behind @wait_until. It takes the condition as a function, which reads well as a do block when the condition is longer than one line. It also takes clock and edge, so it can check on another clock's edges:\n\nstart!(sim, 81, 27)\nwait_until(sim; max_cycles=100) do\n    dut.done == 1 && dut.result > 0\nend\ndut.result\n0x001b","title":"The function form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/waiting/#Which-one-to-use","page":"Waiting for events","text":"To wait for… Use\na fixed number of cycles @tick sim n\na condition on one or more signals @wait_until sim cond max_cycles=…\none signal to rise, fall or change wait_edge(sim, :port; edge=…)\na condition that spans several lines wait_until(sim; max_cycles=…) do … end\n\nAll of them work inside @testbench tasks, and the clock waits for them in lockstep like it does for @tick.\n\n\n\nNext: Testing with Test.jl.","title":"Which one to use"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ports/#Ports-and-wide-signals","page":"Ports and wide signals","text":"Every top-level port of a DUT is a property. Inputs can be read and written; outputs can only be read.","title":"Ports and wide signals"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ports/#Types","page":"Ports and wide signals","text":"Reads return the smallest unsigned type that holds the port, which is also how Verilator stores it:\n\nPort width Julia type\n1–8 bits UInt8\n9–16 bits UInt16\n17–32 bits UInt32\n33–64 bits UInt64\nover 64 bits BigInt\n\nusing VeriJul\n\ndir = joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"counter\")\ndut = load_dut([joinpath(dir, \"counter.v\"), joinpath(dir, \"inverter.v\")], \"counter\";\n               include_dirs=[joinpath(dir, \"include\")], build_dir=mktempdir())\nsettle!(dut)\n(typeof(dut.step), typeof(dut.slow_count), typeof(dut.upper), typeof(dut.wide_out))\n(UInt8, UInt16, UInt64, BigInt)","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ports/#Writing-values","page":"Ports and wide signals","text":"Writes accept any Integer, including Bool, and are truncated to the port width like a Verilog assignment. Negative numbers are stored as two's complement:\n\ndut.step = 300          # 8-bit port: 300 & 0xff\na = dut.step\ndut.step = -1\nb = dut.step\ndut.step = true\nc = dut.step\n(a, b, c)\n(0x2c, 0xff, 0x01)\n\nWriting an output, an unknown port or a non-integer is an error:\n\ntry\n    dut.count = 1\ncatch err\n    err\nend\nErrorException(\"Port `count` is an output and cannot be written\")","title":"Writing values"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ports/#Ports-wider-than-64-bits","page":"Ports and wide signals","text":"Wide ports read back as BigInt and accept any integer. wide_out is the bitwise inverse of the 100-bit wide_in, and upper is its top 64 bits:\n\nx = big(2)^99 + big(2)^64 + 0xCAFE\ndut.wide_in = x\nsettle!(dut)\n(wide_out = string(dut.wide_out, base=16),\n expected = string((big(2)^100 - 1) ⊻ x, base=16),\n upper = dut.upper)\n(wide_out = \"7fffffffeffffffffffff3501\", expected = \"7fffffffeffffffffffff3501\", upper = 0x8000000010000000)\n\nUnder the hood a wide port is an array of 32-bit words, lowest word first. Bit i of the value is bit i of the port.","title":"Ports wider than 64 bits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ports/#Setting-inputs-and-reading-outputs-in-time","page":"Ports and wide signals","text":"Setting an input doesn't evaluate the design. The new value takes effect at the next clock edge (when the simulator evaluates the model) or when you call settle!. Reading an output right after @tick returns gives its value just after that edge.\n\n","title":"Setting inputs and reading outputs in time"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/ports/#Lower-level-access","page":"Ports and wide signals","text":"build_dut returns the library path and a generated bindings.jl with one function per port (set_<port>!, get_<port>) operating on a VerilatedModel. See Lower-level API.","title":"Lower-level access"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/low_level/#Lower-level-API","page":"Lower-level API","text":"load_dut and DUT cover most uses. Underneath them is a smaller interface that works on a bare VerilatedModel and a generated bindings file. It is useful when you want the build step and the loading step separate, or want to see exactly what VeriJul generates.\n\nWith load_dut Lower-level equivalent\ndut = load_dut(src, \"top\") lib, b = build_dut(src, \"top\"); include(b); using .AutoBindings; m = VerilatedModel(lib)\ndut.in_data = 0x42 AutoBindings.set_in_data!(m, 0x42)\ndut.out_data AutoBindings.get_out_data(m)\nenable_trace!(dut, \"w.vcd\") AutoBindings.enable_trace!(m, \"w.vcd\") or enable_trace!(m, \"w.vcd\")\nSimulator(dut) Simulator(m)\n\nbuild_dut returns the path of the compiled library and of the generated bindings.jl. That file defines a module called AutoBindings with a get_<port> function for every port and a set_<port>! function for every input. Values follow the same rules as DUT properties (see Ports and wide signals).","title":"Lower-level API"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/low_level/#Limits","page":"Lower-level API","text":"Every design's generated module is called AutoBindings, so including the bindings of a second design replaces the first. load_dut doesn't generate a module, so any number of designs can be loaded at once.\nFunction lookups in AutoBindings happen on every call. DUT looks them up once when the design is loaded.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/low_level/#Simulator-fields","page":"Lower-level API","text":"sim.rising_edge and sim.falling_edge are the Conditions that the main clock notifies on each edge, and sim.clk_val is the main clock's current value. tick is usually more convenient than waiting on them directly. Waiting on them directly still keeps the clock in lockstep, but the clock only notices such waits by checking every 10 ms, so tick is also faster.","title":"Simulator fields"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Loading-designs","page":"API","text":"","title":"Loading designs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Simulation","page":"API","text":"","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Waiting-and-testing","page":"API","text":"","title":"Waiting and testing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Verification-components","page":"API","text":"","title":"Verification components"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internal-signal-access","page":"API","text":"","title":"Internal signal access"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Coverage-and-random-stimulus","page":"API","text":"","title":"Coverage and random stimulus"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Building","page":"API","text":"","title":"Building"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Low-level-model-access","page":"API","text":"","title":"Low-level model access"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Device.load_dut","page":"API","text":"load_dut(sources, top_module; kwargs...) -> DUT\n\nBuilds the design (or reuses a cached build) and loads it. Takes the same keywords as build_dut: build_dir, include_dirs, defines, parameters, verilator_args, internals, force, verbose. With internals=true, internal signals can be read and written too (see signal).\n\ndut = load_dut(\"filter.v\", \"filter\")\ndut.in_data = 0xFF\n\nBy default the build goes into VeriJul's build cache, one folder per design and set of options, so nothing is written to the current directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Device.load_dut"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Device.DUT","page":"API","text":"DUT\n\nA loaded hardware design. Read and write its ports as properties:\n\ndut.in_data = 0x42      # set an input (truncated to the port width)\ndut.out_data            # read an output (or an input)\n\nPorts up to 64 bits read back as UInt8/UInt16/UInt32/UInt64; wider ports read back as BigInt and accept any Integer.\n\nCreate one with load_dut. List its ports with ports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Device.DUT"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Device.ports","page":"API","text":"ports(dut) -> Vector{HardwarePort}\n\nThe design's top-level ports, in declaration order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Device.ports"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Interop.settle!","page":"API","text":"settle!(model)\n\nEvaluates the model without writing a waveform sample, so combinational outputs reflect inputs you just set. Useful for designs without a clock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Interop.settle!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Interop.enable_trace!","page":"API","text":"enable_trace!(model, filename)\n\nStarts writing a VCD waveform to filename. The file is flushed and closed by destroy!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Interop.enable_trace!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Interop.destroy!","page":"API","text":"destroy!(model)\n\nFrees the C++ model, closes its VCD file and unloads the library. Safe to call more than once. Stop any running clock with stop_clock! first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Interop.destroy!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.Simulator","page":"API","text":"Simulator(dut; clock=:clk, half_period=5)\n\nSimulation state for a DUT (or a bare VerilatedModel): the current time and the clocks that drive it.\n\nclock names the main clock input; tick and @tick wait on it unless told otherwise. Add more clocks with add_clock!.\n\nsim.time is the simulation time of the most recent clock edge, and sim.cycle counts the rising edges of the main clock so far.\n\nThe clocks run in lockstep with the testbench: simulated time only advances while testbench code is waiting in tick, so printing, sleeping or any other I/O between ticks never skips an edge. Run concurrent testbenches with @testbench so the clock waits for all of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.Simulator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.add_clock!","page":"API","text":"add_clock!(sim, name; half_period=5, phase=0)\n\nAdds another 1-bit clock input. It toggles every half_period time units. All clocks run on the same timeline, so their edges interleave correctly. Wait for its edges with tick(sim; clock=name).\n\nphase is measured from the current simulation time: before the simulation has started, the first toggle is at time phase; if clocks are added to a stopped simulation, the first toggle is phase after sim.time (or one half_period after it when phase is 0), so time never goes backwards. The simulator must not be running.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.add_clock!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.start_clock!","page":"API","text":"start_clock!(sim[, half_period]) -> Task\n\nStarts the clocks in a background task. Whenever the testbench is waiting in tick, it toggles the clocks due at the earliest time, evaluates the design (writing a waveform sample if tracing is on) and wakes the waiting tasks. Simulated time does not advance while the testbench is busy, so no edge is ever skipped.\n\nPassing half_period changes the main clock's half period. Throws if the clock is already running.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.start_clock!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.stop_clock!","page":"API","text":"stop_clock!(sim)\n\nStops the clocks and waits for the background task to finish. Tasks still waiting in tick get a ClockStopped error instead of hanging. Call this before destroy!-ing the design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.stop_clock!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.tick","page":"API","text":"tick(sim, n=1; clock=nothing, edge=:rising, timeout=nothing)\n\nWaits for n edges of a clock (the main clock unless clock is given). edge is :rising or :falling. timeout is a wall-clock limit in seconds; when it passes, a TickTimeout is thrown.\n\nSimulated time only advances while testbench code is waiting here, so whatever the task does between ticks (printing, sleeping, I/O) never makes it miss an edge.\n\nThrows ClockStopped if the clock isn't running. @tick is shorthand for this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.tick"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#VeriJul.DSL.@tick","page":"API","text":"@tick sim\n@tick sim n\n@tick sim n clock=:clk_b edge=:falling timeout=1.0\n\nWaits for the next rising edge of the main clock, or n edges. Keywords are passed to tick: clock, edge and timeout (wall-clock seconds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.DSL.@tick"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.reset!","page":"API","text":"reset!(sim, port=:rst; cycles=2, active_high=true)\n\nAsserts a reset input for cycles rising edges of the main clock, then releases it. The clock must be running. Use active_high=false for an active-low reset such as rst_n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.reset!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.wait_until","page":"API","text":"wait_until(cond, sim; max_cycles=nothing, clock=nothing, edge=:rising, timeout=nothing) -> Int\n\nWaits until cond() returns true, checking it now and then after every rising edge of the main clock (or the edge of clock). Returns the number of edges waited, so 0 means the condition already held.\n\nmax_cycles limits how many edges to wait: when it runs out, a WaitTimeout with the simulation time is thrown. timeout is a wall-clock limit in seconds, as for tick.\n\nwait_until(sim; max_cycles=100) do\n    dut.valid == 1\nend\n\n@wait_until is shorthand that also puts the condition's source text in the error message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.wait_until"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#VeriJul.DSL.@wait_until","page":"API","text":"@wait_until sim condition\n@wait_until sim condition max_cycles=100 clock=:clk_b edge=:falling timeout=1.0\n\nWaits until condition (any Julia expression) is true, checking it now and after every rising edge of the main clock. Returns the number of edges waited. Keywords are passed to wait_until; when max_cycles runs out, the WaitTimeout message quotes the condition.\n\n@wait_until sim dut.valid == 1 max_cycles=100\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.DSL.@wait_until"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.wait_edge","page":"API","text":"wait_edge(sim, port; edge=:rising, max_cycles=nothing, clock=nothing, timeout=nothing)\n\nWaits for a port of the design to change, and returns its new value. edge is :rising (0 → 1) or :falling (1 → 0) for 1-bit ports, or :change for any change of a port of any width.\n\nThe port is sampled after every evaluation of the design, that is after each edge of any clock, since that is when outputs change. Inputs set by another testbench are seen at the next evaluation.\n\nmax_cycles counts rising edges of the main clock (or of clock); when it runs out a WaitTimeout is thrown. timeout is a wall-clock limit in seconds. Needs a simulator built from a DUT.\n\nwait_edge(sim, :done)                     # wait for `done` to go high\nwait_edge(sim, :state; edge=:change)      # wait for the FSM to move\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.wait_edge"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#VeriJul.DSL.@testbench","page":"API","text":"@testbench func\n\nDefines func as usual and also a launch_<func>(sim::Simulator, args...; kwargs...) that runs func(sim, args...; kwargs...) as an asynchronous task, so it can wait on clock edges with @tick.\n\nThe clock waits for every launched testbench: no edge happens while any of them is busy between two ticks, so a driver and a monitor running together both see every edge. A finished testbench no longer holds the clock back.\n\n@test inside a testbench records into the @testset that was active when it was launched, and failures show the simulation time and cycle. If the function throws, the task fails with a TestbenchError saying when; wait on the task inside a @testset to report it.\n\n@testbench function drive(sim)\n    sim.dut.in_data = 0xFF\n    @tick sim\nend\n\ntask = launch_drive(sim)\nwait(task)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.DSL.@testbench"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.launch","page":"API","text":"launch(f, sim, args...; kwargs...) -> Task\n\nRuns f(sim, args...; kwargs...) as a testbench task that the clock waits for: no edge happens while it is busy between two ticks. @testbench launchers use this.\n\nThe task records @test results into the @testset that was active when it was launched, adding the simulation time and cycle to every failure. If f throws, the task fails with a TestbenchError saying when.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.launch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.WaitTimeout","page":"API","text":"WaitTimeout\n\nThrown by wait_until, @wait_until and wait_edge when what they wait for doesn't happen within max_cycles clock cycles. Fields: what (a description), cycles (the limit), time and cycle (the simulation time and cycle count when it gave up).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.WaitTimeout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.TestbenchError","page":"API","text":"TestbenchError\n\nThe error a @testbench task fails with when its function throws. It says which testbench failed and when. Fields: testbench (the function name), time and cycle (the simulation time and cycle count), error (the original exception) and backtrace.\n\nwait(task) and fetch(task) throw a TaskFailedException wrapping it, which a surrounding @testset reports as an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.TestbenchError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.ClockStopped","page":"API","text":"ClockStopped()\n\nThrown by tick when the clock isn't running, or stops while a task is waiting for an edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.ClockStopped"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.SimCore.TickTimeout","page":"API","text":"TickTimeout(seconds)\n\nThrown by tick when the requested edges don't arrive within timeout seconds of wall-clock time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.SimCore.TickTimeout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.StreamBus","page":"API","text":"StreamBus(; data, valid, ready=nothing, clock=nothing)\n\nNames the ports of one valid/ready (AXI-Stream style) interface: a word moves on a rising clock edge when valid and ready are both high. ready=nothing means the interface has no backpressure (always ready). clock is the clock the interface belongs to (the main clock by default).\n\ninp = StreamBus(data=:s_data, valid=:s_valid, ready=:s_ready)\nout = StreamBus(data=:m_data, valid=:m_valid, ready=:m_ready)\n\nUse it with send!, receive! and StreamMonitor. They all sample the handshake at the falling edge before each rising edge, when the inputs set after the previous rising edge have settled, so they see exactly the values the design sees at the edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.StreamBus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.send!","page":"API","text":"send!(sim, bus, items; idle=0.0, rng=Random.default_rng(), max_cycles=nothing)\n\nDrives items into the design through bus, one word per handshake, and returns once the last one is accepted. Holds each word with valid high until the design is ready, and leaves valid low afterwards.\n\nidle (from 0 up to, but not including, 1) is the probability of inserting an idle cycle (valid low) before each word, to test that the design copes with gaps. max_cycles limits how long one word may wait for ready; then a WaitTimeout is thrown.\n\nCall it from a @testbench task (or the main task) while the clock runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.send!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.receive!","page":"API","text":"receive!(sim, bus, n; ready=1.0, rng=Random.default_rng(), max_cycles=nothing) -> Vector\n\nReceives n words from the design through bus and returns them. Drives the bus's ready input: with ready < 1 (it must be above 0), ready is high in each cycle only with that probability, to test that the design handles backpressure. ready is left low afterwards.\n\nmax_cycles limits how long to wait for each word; then a WaitTimeout is thrown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.receive!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.StreamMonitor","page":"API","text":"StreamMonitor(sim, bus; on_item=nothing) -> StreamMonitor\n\nWatches bus without driving it, and records every word that moves: in mon.items, with the simulation time of each transfer in mon.times. on_item(x), if given, is called for each word, for example to feed a Scoreboard.\n\nThe monitor runs as a testbench task from the moment it is created. End it with stop!; it also ends quietly when the clock stops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.StreamMonitor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.stop!","page":"API","text":"stop!(mon::StreamMonitor) -> Vector\n\nStops the monitor after the current cycle and returns the words it saw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.stop!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.Scoreboard","page":"API","text":"Scoreboard(name=\"\"; sim=nothing, compare=isequal)\n\nChecks that a design produces the expected values, in order. Queue what should come out with expect!, report what did come out with observe!, and check the result with @test passed(sb). Showing the scoreboard lists every mismatch, with the simulation time when sim is given.\n\nsb = Scoreboard(\"fifo\"; sim)\nexpect!(sb, data...)\nmon = StreamMonitor(sim, out; on_item = x -> observe!(sb, x))\n...\n@test passed(sb)\n\ncompare(expected, got) decides whether two values match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.Scoreboard"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.expect!","page":"API","text":"expect!(sb, values...) -> sb\n\nQueues values the design should produce next, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.expect!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.observe!","page":"API","text":"observe!(sb, value) -> Bool\n\nCompares a value the design produced with the oldest expected one, records a mismatch if they differ (or if nothing was expected), and returns whether it matched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.observe!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Components.passed","page":"API","text":"passed(sb) -> Bool\n\ntrue when every observed value matched and nothing expected is still outstanding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Components.passed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Device.signal","page":"API","text":"signal(dut, name) -> Signal\n\nLooks up a signal anywhere in the design by its hierarchical name, relative to the top module: \"count\", \"u_core.state\", \"inv.y\". The design must be loaded with load_dut(...; internals=true). signals lists the names.\n\ndut = load_dut(\"fifo.v\", \"fifo\"; internals=true)\noccupancy = signal(dut, \"count\")\noccupancy[]          # read\noccupancy[] = 0      # write\n\ndut[\"count\"] and dut[\"count\"] = 0 are shorthand for one-off access; keep the Signal when reading the same signal often, since the lookup is the slow part. Parameters can be read but not written; arrays and memories (reg [7:0] mem [0:3]) are listed by signals but can't be accessed yet.\n\nA write deposits a value: it holds until the design's own logic next assigns the signal (for a register, typically the next clock edge). Call settle! to update logic that depends on it before the next edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Device.signal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Device.signals","page":"API","text":"signals(dut) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe hierarchical names of all signals in the design (ports included), relative to the top module, for use with signal. Needs a design loaded with internals=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Device.signals"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Device.Signal","page":"API","text":"Signal\n\nA handle to a signal anywhere in the design hierarchy, from signal. Read it with sig[] and write it with sig[] = value. Values have the same types as ports: UInt8 to UInt64 up to 64 bits, BigInt above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Device.Signal"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Coverage.CoverGroup","page":"API","text":"CoverGroup(name, :point => bins, ...; cross=[])\n\nFunctional coverage: which interesting cases a test has actually exercised. Each cover point names a value you sample! and lists its bins. A bin is\n\n\"label\" => matcher, where the matcher is a range or collection (0:15, [1, 2, 4]), a predicate (iseven, x -> x > 100) or a value;\nor just a value, which is its own bin (0, :read).\n\nbins splits a range into equal bins. cross lists tuples of point names whose combinations of bins should be covered.\n\ncg = CoverGroup(\"gcd inputs\",\n    :a => [\"zero\" => 0, \"small\" => 1:15, \"large\" => 16:65535],\n    :b => [\"zero\" => 0, \"small\" => 1:15, \"large\" => 16:65535],\n    :equal => [true, false];\n    cross = [(:a, :b)])\n\nsample!(cg; a = 12, b = 300, equal = false)\ncoverage(cg)      # fraction of bins hit, 0.0 to 1.0\nuncovered(cg)     # what is still missing\n\nA sample counts towards every bin it matches. Showing the group prints a report with the hits per bin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Coverage.CoverGroup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Coverage.sample!","page":"API","text":"sample!(cg; point=value, ...) -> cg\nsample!(cg, (point=value, ...)) -> cg\n\nRecords one sample: each named point's value is matched against its bins. Every point of the group must be given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Coverage.sample!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Coverage.coverage","page":"API","text":"coverage(cg) -> Float64\ncoverage(cg, point) -> Float64\n\nThe fraction of bins (and cross combinations) that have been hit at least once, from 0.0 to 1.0; or the fraction for one cover point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Coverage.coverage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Coverage.uncovered","page":"API","text":"uncovered(cg) -> Vector{String}\n\nDescriptions of the bins and cross combinations not hit yet, such as \"a: zero\" or \"a × b: (zero, large)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Coverage.uncovered"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Coverage.bins","page":"API","text":"bins(range, n) -> Vector{Pair{String,UnitRange}}\n\nSplits range into n bins of (nearly) equal size, labelled by their bounds: bins(0:255, 4) gives \"0:63\" => 0:63, …, \"192:255\" => 192:255.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Coverage.bins"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Coverage.test_seed","page":"API","text":"test_seed(; env=\"VERIJUL_SEED\") -> UInt64\n\nA seed for a test's random number generator, logged so a failing run can be reproduced. Takes the seed from the environment variable env if it is set, and picks a fresh random one otherwise.\n\nusing Random\nrng = Xoshiro(test_seed())\n# [ Info: Random seed 0x5f3a…; rerun with VERIJUL_SEED=0x5f3a… to reproduce\n\nPass the rng to everything random in the test (rand(rng, …), send!, …) so the whole run repeats.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Coverage.test_seed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.build_dut","page":"API","text":"build_dut(sources, top_module; kwargs...) -> (lib_path, bindings_path)\n\nVerilates sources (one path or a vector of paths) with top_module as the top, generates bindings and compiles everything into a shared library. Returns the library path and the path of the generated bindings.jl.\n\nMost code should call load_dut, which builds and loads in one step and takes the same keywords.\n\nKeywords\n\nbuild_dir: where build files go. By default each design and set of options gets its own folder in VeriJul's build cache (a Scratch.jl space, removed with clear_build_cache or when VeriJul is uninstalled). A directory you pass must be empty, new, or one VeriJul created earlier; VeriJul never deletes files it didn't create.\ninclude_dirs=String[]: directories searched by include (-I).\ndefines=Dict(): preprocessor macros, e.g. Dict(\"DEPTH\" => 16, \"FAST\" => nothing) (+define+).\nparameters=Dict(): top-level parameter overrides, e.g. Dict(\"WIDTH\" => 12) (-G). Values are passed to Verilator as written, so string parameters need their own quotes: \"\\\"name\\\"\".\nverilator_args=String[]: extra command-line arguments for Verilator.\ninternals=false: also make internal signals (not just ports) readable and writable, with signal and dut[\"path\"]. This passes --vpi --public-flat-rw to Verilator, which makes the model slower and the build bigger, so it is off by default.\nforce=false: rebuild even if nothing changed.\nverbose=false: show Verilator and compiler output.\n\nBuilds are cached: if the sources, files in include_dirs, options, Verilator version and compiler are unchanged, the existing library is reused. To rebuild, VeriJul empties the build directory first. Files pulled in by include from outside include_dirs are not tracked; pass force=true after changing them.\n\nSet the CXX environment variable to choose the C++ compiler (default g++, or c++ on macOS; CXX=\"ccache g++\" also works). Set VERIJUL_VERILATOR to choose the Verilator executable (see verilator_cmd).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.build_dut"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.clear_build_cache","page":"API","text":"clear_build_cache()\n\nDeletes all cached builds in VeriJul's default build location (used when build_dir is not given). Designs that are still loaded keep working.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.clear_build_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.verilator_cmd","page":"API","text":"verilator_cmd() -> Cmd\n\nThe Verilator command VeriJul runs. Set the VERIJUL_VERILATOR environment variable to the path of a verilator executable to use that one; otherwise verilator is looked up on PATH. Throws an error explaining how to install Verilator if none is found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.verilator_cmd"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.verilator_version","page":"API","text":"verilator_version() -> VersionNumber\n\nReturns the version of the Verilator that verilator_cmd finds, or throws an error explaining how to install it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.verilator_version"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.HardwarePort","page":"API","text":"HardwarePort(name, direction, width)\n\nA top-level port of a Verilated design. direction is :in or :out (inout ports are treated as :in), width is the bit width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.HardwarePort"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.extract_ports","page":"API","text":"extract_ports(header_path) -> Vector{HardwarePort}\n\nReads the ports of a Verilated model from its generated V<top>.h header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.extract_ports"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Builder.generate_bindings","page":"API","text":"generate_bindings(ports, module_name; internals=false) -> (cpp_code, jl_code)\n\nGenerates the C++ shim exposing the model through a C ABI, and the Julia AutoBindings module that wraps it (set_<port>! / get_<port>). Every port gets a getter; input ports also get a setter. With internals=true the shim also exposes internal signals through Verilator's VPI (the design must be Verilated with --vpi --public-flat-rw).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Builder.generate_bindings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#VeriJul.Interop.VerilatedModel","page":"API","text":"VerilatedModel(lib_path, create_sym=:top_create)\n\nLoads a shared library built by build_dut and instantiates the model. The model is freed automatically when garbage-collected, or explicitly with destroy!.\n\nMost code should use load_dut instead, which returns a DUT with named port access.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Interop.VerilatedModel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#VeriJul.Interop.eval_step!","page":"API","text":"eval_step!(model, time)\n\nEvaluates the model and, if tracing is enabled, dumps a waveform sample at time. The simulator's clock calls this for you on every clock edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VeriJul.Interop.eval_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/build_and_clocks/#Build-options-and-multiple-clocks","page":"Build options and multiple clocks","text":"Real designs span several files, use include and define, have parameters, and often more than one clock. This tutorial uses the counter in examples/counter:\n\nexamples/counter/\n├── counter.v          top module, `include \"consts.vh\"\n├── inverter.v         submodule\n└── include/consts.vh  defines INIT_VAL (default 8'd7)\n\nmodule counter #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (\n    input  wire             clk,        // count += step on each rising edge\n    input  wire             clk_slow,   // slow_count += 1 on each rising edge\n    input  wire             rst,\n    input  wire [WIDTH-1:0] step,\n    input  wire [99:0]      wide_in,\n    output wire [99:0]      wide_out,   // ~wide_in\n    output wire [63:0]      upper,      // wide_in[99:36]\n    output wire [7:0]       init_val,   // `INIT_VAL\n    output reg  [WIDTH-1:0] count,\n    output reg  [15:0]      slow_count\n);","title":"Build options and multiple clocks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/build_and_clocks/#Multiple-files-and-include-directories","page":"Build options and multiple clocks","text":"Pass a vector of sources and the directories include should search:\n\nusing VeriJul\n\ndir = joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"counter\")\nsrcs = [joinpath(dir, \"counter.v\"), joinpath(dir, \"inverter.v\")]\nincs = [joinpath(dir, \"include\")]\n\ndut = load_dut(srcs, \"counter\"; include_dirs=incs, build_dir=mktempdir())\nDUT(counter, 10 ports)\n  input   clk              1 bit\n  input   clk_slow         1 bit\n  input   rst              1 bit\n  input   step             8 bits\n  output  init_val         8 bits\n  output  count            8 bits\n  output  slow_count       16 bits\n  input   wide_in          100 bits\n  output  wide_out         100 bits\n  output  upper            64 bits","title":"Multiple files and include directories"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/build_and_clocks/#Parameters-and-defines","page":"Build options and multiple clocks","text":"parameters overrides top-level parameters (Verilator's -G) and defines sets preprocessor macros (+define+). Each combination is its own build, and each is cached:\n\nwide = load_dut(srcs, \"counter\"; include_dirs=incs, build_dir=mktempdir(),\n                parameters=Dict(\"WIDTH\" => 12),\n                defines=Dict(\"INIT_VAL\" => \"8'd42\"))\nsettle!(dut); settle!(wide)\n(default_init = dut.init_val, overridden_init = wide.init_val,\n count_width = only(p.width for p in ports(wide) if p.name == \"count\"))\n(default_init = 0x07, overridden_init = 0x2a, count_width = 12)\n\nsettle! evaluates the design without a clock edge, so combinational outputs like init_val update. Use defines=Dict(\"NAME\" => nothing) for a macro without a value, and verilator_args for any other Verilator flag.","title":"Parameters and defines"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/build_and_clocks/#Build-caching","page":"Build options and multiple clocks","text":"load_dut and build_dut skip the build when the sources, the files in include_dirs, the options, the Verilator version and the compiler are all unchanged. Pass force=true to rebuild anyway, for example after editing a file that is included from outside include_dirs, and verbose=true to see Verilator's output.","title":"Build caching"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/build_and_clocks/#Where-builds-go","page":"Build options and multiple clocks","text":"Without build_dir, each design and set of options gets its own folder in VeriJul's build cache (a Scratch.jl space in your Julia depot), so nothing is written to the current directory and cached builds survive between sessions. clear_build_cache deletes them, and they are removed when VeriJul is uninstalled.\n\nIf you pass build_dir, it must be new, empty, or a directory VeriJul created before (VeriJul marks those with a .verijul-build file). VeriJul refuses to use a non-empty directory it didn't create, so it never deletes your files.\n\n","title":"Where builds go"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/build_and_clocks/#Two-clocks","page":"Build options and multiple clocks","text":"The Simulator drives clk by default. Add other clocks with add_clock! before starting. Here clk has a period of 10 and clk_slow a period of 30:\n\nsim = Simulator(dut)                         # clk: half period 5\nadd_clock!(sim, :clk_slow; half_period=15)\nstart_clock!(sim)\n\nreset!(sim)\ndut.step = 1\nfor _ in 1:7\n    @tick sim\n    println(\"t = \", lpad(sim.time, 3), \"   count = \", lpad(Int(dut.count), 2),\n            \"   slow_count = \", dut.slow_count)\nend\nt =  20   count =  1   slow_count = 0\nt =  30   count =  2   slow_count = 1\nt =  40   count =  3   slow_count = 1\nt =  50   count =  4   slow_count = 1\nt =  60   count =  5   slow_count = 2\nt =  70   count =  6   slow_count = 2\nt =  80   count =  7   slow_count = 2\n\nAll clocks share one timeline, so their edges interleave correctly: the simulator always advances to the earliest pending edge, toggles every clock due at that time, and evaluates the design once.\n\nWait for a specific clock with clock=, and for falling edges with edge=:falling:\n\n@tick sim 2 clock=:clk_slow\n(time = sim.time, slow_count = dut.slow_count)\n(time = 120, slow_count = 0x0004)\n\n@tick sim edge=:falling\nsim.time\n125\n\nUse phase to offset a clock: add_clock!(sim, :clk_b; half_period=5, phase=2) first toggles clk_b at time 2.\n\n\n\nNext: Ports and wide signals.","title":"Two clocks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/streams/#Streams-and-scoreboards","page":"Streams and scoreboards","text":"Most designs talk through standard interfaces, and the most common one is a valid/ready stream (the handshake used by AXI-Stream): the sender raises valid when it has a word, the receiver raises ready when it can take one, and the word moves on a rising clock edge when both are high. Writing the driver for that by hand in every testbench is tedious and easy to get subtly wrong, so VeriJul has components for it:\n\nStreamBus names the ports of one interface.\nsend! drives words into the design, waiting for ready.\nreceive! takes words out, driving ready.\nStreamMonitor passively records every word that moves.\nScoreboard checks that the right words come out, in order.\n\nThis tutorial tests examples/fifo.v, a 4-word FIFO with a stream on each side.\n\nusing VeriJul, Test, Random\n\ndut = load_dut(joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"fifo.v\"), \"fifo\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nreset!(sim)\ndut\nDUT(fifo, 8 ports)\n  input   clk              1 bit\n  input   rst              1 bit\n  input   s_data           8 bits\n  input   s_valid          1 bit\n  output  s_ready          1 bit\n  output  m_data           8 bits\n  output  m_valid          1 bit\n  input   m_ready          1 bit","title":"Streams and scoreboards"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/streams/#Describing-the-interfaces","page":"Streams and scoreboards","text":"A StreamBus just names the ports. Interfaces without backpressure leave out ready.\n\ninp = StreamBus(data=:s_data, valid=:s_valid, ready=:s_ready)\nout = StreamBus(data=:m_data, valid=:m_valid, ready=:m_ready)","title":"Describing the interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/streams/#Sending-and-receiving","page":"Streams and scoreboards","text":"send! returns once the design has accepted every word, and receive! returns the words once it has n of them:\n\nsend!(sim, inp, [0x11, 0x22, 0x33])\nreceive!(sim, out, 3)\n3-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0x11\n 0x22\n 0x33\n\nThe FIFO holds four words. With nobody reading, a fifth word is never accepted. max_cycles turns that into an error instead of a hang:\n\nsend!(sim, inp, [0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x04])\ntry\n    send!(sim, inp, [0x05]; max_cycles=10)\ncatch err\n    sprint(showerror, err)\nend\n\"WaitTimeout: `s_ready` accepting a word did not happen within 10 cycles (gave up at time 210, cycle 22)\"\n\nreceive!(sim, out, 4)\n4-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0x01\n 0x02\n 0x03\n 0x04","title":"Sending and receiving"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/streams/#Gaps-and-backpressure","page":"Streams and scoreboards","text":"A design that works when data arrives every cycle can still break when it doesn't. send! inserts idle cycles with probability idle, and receive! only raises ready with probability ready, so the design sees irregular traffic and full and empty states. Both take an rng, so the traffic can be reproduced (see Random stimulus and coverage).\n\nSender and receiver run at the same time, so start them as @testbench tasks:\n\n@testbench tx(sim, data, rng) = send!(sim, inp, data; idle=0.3, rng)\n@testbench rx(sim, n, rng) = receive!(sim, out, n; ready=0.4, rng)\n\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\ndata = rand(rng, UInt8, 50)\nt = launch_tx(sim, data, rng)\nr = launch_rx(sim, length(data), rng)\nwait(t)\nfetch(r) == data\ntrue","title":"Gaps and backpressure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/streams/#Monitors-and-scoreboards","page":"Streams and scoreboards","text":"In bigger testbenches, the code that drives a design and the code that checks it are kept apart. A StreamMonitor watches an interface without driving it, and calls on_item for every word that moves. Connecting it to a Scoreboard checks the output against what you expect:\n\nsb = Scoreboard(\"fifo\"; sim)\nexpect!(sb, data...)                        # a FIFO returns its input in order\nmon = StreamMonitor(sim, out; on_item = x -> observe!(sb, x))\n\nt = launch_tx(sim, data, rng)\nr = launch_rx(sim, length(data), rng)\nwait(t); wait(r)\nstop!(mon)\nsb\nScoreboard(fifo: 50 matched, 0 mismatched, 0 still expected)\n\nUse @test passed(sb) in a test. When something goes wrong, showing the scoreboard lists the mismatches with their simulation time:\n\nbad = Scoreboard(\"demo\"; sim)\nexpect!(bad, 1, 2, 3)\nobserve!(bad, 1); observe!(bad, 7)\nbad\nScoreboard(demo: 1 matched, 1 mismatched, 1 still expected)\n  #2: expected 2, got 7 (time 3290)\n  never seen: 3\n\nScoreboard(; compare = (expected, got) -> ...) changes what counts as a match, for example to allow rounding errors.","title":"Monitors and scoreboards"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/streams/#How-the-timing-works","page":"Streams and scoreboards","text":"All stream components look at the handshake at the falling edge just before each rising edge. By then the inputs set after the previous rising edge have been evaluated, so they see exactly the values the design sees at the edge. Words move at rising edges as usual. Change a bus's inputs only between a rising edge and the next falling edge (which is what happens when your testbench code runs right after @tick), and the components will agree with the design about which words moved.\n\nFor an interface on another clock, pass StreamBus(...; clock=:clk_b).\n\n\n\nNext: Internal signals.","title":"How the timing works"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/coverage/#Random-stimulus-and-coverage","page":"Random stimulus and coverage","text":"Random inputs find bugs that hand-written tests miss, because they try combinations nobody thought of. Two things make random testing practical:\n\nReproducibility. When a random test fails, you need to run exactly the same inputs again to debug it.\nCoverage. Random doesn't mean thorough. You need to know which interesting cases the inputs actually hit, and which they never did.\n\nThis tutorial uses the GCD unit from Waiting for events.\n\nusing VeriJul, Test, Random\n\ndut = load_dut(joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"gcd.v\"), \"gcd\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nreset!(sim)\n\nfunction run_gcd(sim, a, b)\n    sim.dut.a = a; sim.dut.b = b; sim.dut.start = 1\n    @tick sim\n    sim.dut.start = 0\n    cycles = @wait_until sim sim.dut.done == 1 max_cycles=100_000\n    return sim.dut.result, cycles\nend","title":"Random stimulus and coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/coverage/#Reproducible-random-numbers","page":"Random stimulus and coverage","text":"Give each test its own random number generator, seeded by test_seed. It logs the seed, and takes it from the VERIJUL_SEED environment variable when that is set, so a failing run can be repeated exactly:\n\nrng = Xoshiro(test_seed())\n[ Info: Random seed 0x21eb8db0f599cd88; rerun with VERIJUL_SEED=0x21eb8db0f599cd88 to reproduce\n\nPass rng to everything random in the test: rand(rng, ...), and the rng keyword of send! and receive!. Then a failure in CI can be replayed on your machine with VERIJUL_SEED=0x… julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()', using the seed from the log.\n\n(This page uses a fixed seed, so it shows the same numbers every time it's built.)\n\nrng = Xoshiro(2024)","title":"Reproducible random numbers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/coverage/#Describing-what-to-cover","page":"Random stimulus and coverage","text":"A CoverGroup lists the values to watch and sorts them into bins: the cases that should each happen at least once. For the GCD unit, interesting cases are zero inputs, small and large inputs, equal inputs, and how long the computation took:\n\nsize_bins = [\"zero\" => 0, \"one\" => 1, \"small\" => 2:15, \"medium\" => 16:255, \"large\" => 256:65535]\n\ncg = CoverGroup(\"gcd\",\n    :a => size_bins,\n    :b => size_bins,\n    :equal => [true, false],\n    :cycles => [\"fast\" => 0:4, \"normal\" => 5:50, \"slow\" => x -> x > 50];\n    cross = [(:a, :b)])          # every combination of a's and b's size\n\nA bin is \"label\" => matcher, where the matcher is a range or collection, a predicate like iseven, or a single value, or just a value on its own. bins splits a range into equal parts: bins(0:255, 4).","title":"Describing what to cover"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/coverage/#Sampling","page":"Random stimulus and coverage","text":"Run random inputs and sample! each case:\n\n@testset \"random gcd\" begin\n    for _ in 1:100\n        a, b = rand(rng, UInt16), rand(rng, UInt16)\n        result, cycles = run_gcd(sim, a, b)\n        @test result == gcd(a, b)\n        sample!(cg; a, b, equal = a == b, cycles)\n    end\nend\ncoverage(cg)\n0.15\n\nUniformly random 16-bit numbers are almost always \"large\", so 100 runs left most of the plan untested. Showing the group gives the details:\n\nshow(stdout, MIME\"text/plain\"(), cg)\nCoverGroup(gcd, 15.0% covered, 100 samples)\n  a: 20.0%\n    zero         0  ← not hit\n    one          0  ← not hit\n    small        0  ← not hit\n    medium       0  ← not hit\n    large      100\n  b: 20.0%\n    zero         0  ← not hit\n    one          0  ← not hit\n    small        0  ← not hit\n    medium       0  ← not hit\n    large      100\n  equal: 50.0%\n    true        0  ← not hit\n    false     100\n  cycles: 66.7%\n    fast         0  ← not hit\n    normal      61\n    slow        39\n  a × b: 1 of 25 combinations hit\n\nuncovered lists what's missing:\n\nfirst(uncovered(cg), 8)\n8-element Vector{String}:\n \"a: zero\"\n \"a: one\"\n \"a: small\"\n \"a: medium\"\n \"b: zero\"\n \"b: one\"\n \"b: small\"\n \"b: medium\"","title":"Sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/coverage/#Closing-the-gaps","page":"Random stimulus and coverage","text":"Rather than more of the same, pick inputs that aim at what's missing. Choosing a bin first and then a value inside it spreads the tests across all the cases. Keep going until everything is covered, with a limit so the loop always ends:\n\npick_size(rng) = rand(rng, (0:0, 1:1, 2:15, 16:255, 256:65535))\n\n@testset \"coverage-driven gcd\" begin\n    n = 0\n    while coverage(cg) < 1 && n < 5_000\n        a = rand(rng, pick_size(rng))\n        b = rand(rng) < 0.1 ? a : rand(rng, pick_size(rng))     # sometimes equal\n        result, cycles = run_gcd(sim, a, b)\n        @test result == gcd(a, b)\n        sample!(cg; a, b, equal = a == b, cycles)\n        n += 1\n    end\n    @test coverage(cg) == 1\nend\ncg\nCoverGroup(gcd, 100.0% covered, 159 samples)\n  a: 100.0%\n    zero        10\n    one         11\n    small       14\n    medium      14\n    large      110\n  b: 100.0%\n    zero         8\n    one         11\n    small       17\n    medium      13\n    large      110\n  equal: 100.0%\n    true        8\n    false     151\n  cycles: 100.0%\n    fast        25\n    normal      78\n    slow        56\n  a × b: 25 of 25 combinations hit\n\nEvery bin and every size combination has now been tried at least once, and the design matched Julia's gcd on all of them.\n\nCoverage goals belong in the test itself: a @test coverage(cg) == 1 at the end fails the test run if a change to the stimulus quietly stops reaching a case.\n\n\n\nNext: Build options and multiple clocks.","title":"Closing the gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/internals/#Internal-signals","page":"Internal signals","text":"Ports are what a design shows the outside world, but when you're debugging, or checking that a design does the right thing for the right reason, you often want to see inside it: the state of a state machine, a counter, a pointer. Load the design with internals=true and every signal in the hierarchy can be read and written by its name.\n\nusing VeriJul\n\ndut = load_dut(joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"fifo.v\"), \"fifo\";\n               internals=true, build_dir=mktempdir())\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nreset!(sim)\n\ninternals=true makes Verilator keep every signal accessible (it passes --vpi --public-flat-rw). That makes the model slower and the build bigger, so it is off by default. Turn it on for the test runs that need it.","title":"Internal signals"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/internals/#Finding-signals","page":"Internal signals","text":"signals lists the hierarchical names, relative to the top module. Signals in submodules look like \"u_core.state\".\n\nsignals(dut)\n17-element Vector{String}:\n \"AW\"\n \"DEPTH\"\n \"WIDTH\"\n \"clk\"\n \"count\"\n \"m_data\"\n \"m_ready\"\n \"m_valid\"\n \"mem\"\n \"pop\"\n \"push\"\n \"rd_ptr\"\n \"rst\"\n \"s_data\"\n \"s_ready\"\n \"s_valid\"\n \"wr_ptr\"","title":"Finding signals"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/internals/#Reading","page":"Internal signals","text":"dut[\"name\"] reads a signal once. For a signal you read often, get a Signal handle with signal and read it with sig[], since looking up the name is the slow part:\n\ninp = StreamBus(data=:s_data, valid=:s_valid, ready=:s_ready)\ncount = signal(dut, \"count\")\n\nsend!(sim, inp, [0xA1, 0xA2, 0xA3])\n(count[], dut[\"wr_ptr\"], dut[\"rd_ptr\"])\n(0x03, 0x03, 0x00)\n\nValues have the same types as ports: UInt8 up to UInt64, and BigInt for signals wider than 64 bits.\n\nA natural use is checking an invariant on every cycle, here that the FIFO never claims to hold more than four words:\n\nusing Test\n\n@testbench function occupancy_check(sim, count, n)\n    for _ in 1:n\n        @tick sim\n        @test count[] <= 4\n        @test (sim.dut.s_ready == 0) == (count[] == 4)\n    end\nend\n\nout = StreamBus(data=:m_data, valid=:m_valid, ready=:m_ready)\n@testbench tx(sim, data) = send!(sim, inp, data)\n@testbench rx(sim, n) = receive!(sim, out, n; ready=0.3)   # a slow reader: the FIFO fills up\n\n@testset \"occupancy\" begin\n    c = launch_occupancy_check(sim, count, 40)\n    t = launch_tx(sim, 1:8)\n    r = launch_rx(sim, 11)            # the three from before and the eight new ones\n    wait(t); wait(r); wait(c)\nend\nTest Summary: | Pass  Total  Time\noccupancy     |   80     80  0.4s","title":"Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/internals/#Writing","page":"Internal signals","text":"Writing a signal deposits a value: it stays until the design's own logic assigns that signal again. For a register, that's usually the next clock edge. That's handy for putting a design straight into a state that would take many cycles to reach:\n\ndut[\"count\"] = 2        # pretend two words are stored\nsettle!(dut)            # update the logic that depends on it\n(dut.m_valid, count[])\n(0x01, 0x02)\n\nWithout settle!, outputs that depend on the signal only update at the next clock edge. Parameters and constants can't be written.\n\nreset!(sim)\ncount[]\n0x00\n\n\n\nNext: Random stimulus and coverage.","title":"Writing"},{"category":"section","location":"#VeriJul.jl","page":"Home","text":"VeriJul lets you write hardware testbenches in Julia. It compiles a Verilog design with Verilator, gives you its ports as ordinary Julia properties, and drives the simulation from Julia tasks, so you can check hardware against reference models, random stimulus and everything else in the Julia ecosystem.\n\nusing VeriJul\n\ndut = load_dut(\"filter.v\", \"filter\")      # build (cached) and load\nsim = Simulator(dut)                      # drives the `clk` input\nstart_clock!(sim)\n\ndut.in_data = 0xFF\n@tick sim 6                               # wait for 6 rising edges\ndut.out_data                              # 0xff","title":"VeriJul.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"One-call builds with build caching, multiple source files, include directories, defines and parameter overrides.\nNamed port access: dut.port = value and dut.port, for any width, including ports wider than 64 bits (as BigInt).\nClocks and time: multiple clocks with their own periods and phases, @tick sim n, reset helpers, and timeouts so a stuck testbench fails instead of hanging.\nWaveforms: VCD tracing you can open in GTKWave or Surfer.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Requirements","page":"Home","text":"Julia 1.10 or newer\nVerilator 4.200 or newer on your PATH (or set VERIJUL_VERILATOR to its path)\nA C++17 compiler (g++ or clang++; set CXX to pick one)\n\nVeriJul is tested on Linux and macOS. Windows is not supported yet.\n\n# Ubuntu / Debian\nsudo apt-get install verilator g++\n# macOS\nbrew install verilator","title":"Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"VeriJul\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Getting started walks through building, driving and tracing a small filter.\nSelf-checking testbenches shows how to compare a design against a Julia reference model with Test.\nWaiting for events waits for conditions and signal edges in designs whose latency varies, with cycle limits.\nTesting with Test.jl runs @test inside concurrent testbenches, with failures that report the simulation time.\nStreams and scoreboards drives and checks valid/ready interfaces with random gaps and backpressure.\nInternal signals reads and writes signals inside the design.\nRandom stimulus and coverage makes random tests reproducible and measures which cases they hit.\nBuild options and multiple clocks covers multi-file designs, parameters, defines and clock domains.\nPorts and wide signals explains port types, truncation and ports wider than 64 bits.\n\nTo keep building and loading as separate steps, see Lower-level API.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This tutorial builds a small Verilog design, drives it from Julia and records a waveform. It uses the 4-tap moving-average filter from examples/filter.v:\n\nmodule filter (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire [7:0] in_data,\n    output reg  [7:0] out_data\n);\n\nOn every rising edge of clk it shifts in_data into a 4-entry history and outputs the average of the previous four samples.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Build-and-load-the-design","page":"Getting started","text":"load_dut runs Verilator, compiles the result and loads it. Builds are cached, so running it again with the same sources is instant.\n\nusing VeriJul\n\nsrc = joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"filter.v\")\ndut = load_dut(src, \"filter\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nDUT(filter, 3 ports)\n  input   clk              1 bit\n  input   in_data          8 bits\n  output  out_data         8 bits\n\nThe DUT shows its ports. You can also list them with ports:\n\nports(dut)\n3-element Vector{HardwarePort}:\n HardwarePort(\"clk\", :in, 1)\n HardwarePort(\"in_data\", :in, 8)\n HardwarePort(\"out_data\", :out, 8)","title":"Build and load the design"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Drive-the-clock","page":"Getting started","text":"A Simulator owns simulation time and toggles the clk input in a background task once you call start_clock!. By default the clock has a half period of 5 time units, so rising edges arrive every 10.\n\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nTask (runnable, started) @0x00007f6b1cd1d960\n\n@tick waits for rising edges. Set an input, wait, and read the output:\n\ndut.in_data = 0xFF\nfor cycle in 1:6\n    @tick sim\n    println(\"t = \", sim.time, \"   out_data = \", dut.out_data)\nend\nt = 0   out_data = 0\nt = 10   out_data = 0\nt = 20   out_data = 63\nt = 30   out_data = 127\nt = 40   out_data = 191\nt = 50   out_data = 255\n\nThe clock runs in lockstep with your code: simulated time only advances while you are waiting in @tick. Printing, sleeping or any other work between ticks never makes you miss an edge, which is why the times above are exactly 10 apart.\n\nThe output ramps up over four cycles as the history fills with 0xFF, one cycle later than you might expect because out_data is itself a register.\n\nInputs take any integer and are truncated to the port width, just like a Verilog assignment:\n\ndut.in_data = 0x142    # 9 bits; only the low 8 are kept\ndut.in_data\n0x42","title":"Drive the clock"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Record-a-waveform","page":"Getting started","text":"Call enable_trace! before simulating to write a VCD file. Each clock edge adds a sample at sim.time. The file is flushed when the design is destroyed.\n\nstop_clock!(sim)\ndestroy!(dut)\n\ndut = load_dut(src, \"filter\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nvcd = joinpath(mktempdir(), \"filter.vcd\")\nenable_trace!(dut, vcd)\n\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\nfor x in (0x10, 0x80, 0xFF, 0x00, 0x40)\n    dut.in_data = x\n    @tick sim 2\nend\nstop_clock!(sim)\ndestroy!(dut)\n\nfilesize(vcd)\n1261\n\nOpen the file in GTKWave or Surfer to see clk, in_data, out_data and the internal shift register.","title":"Record a waveform"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Clean-up","page":"Getting started","text":"Always stop the clock before destroying a design: the clock task calls into the model on every edge. stop_clock! waits for that task to finish. A design that is garbage-collected is destroyed automatically, but calling destroy! yourself makes sure the waveform is written.\n\nNext: Self-checking testbenches.","title":"Clean up"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testbenches/#Self-checking-testbenches","page":"Self-checking testbenches","text":"Printing values is fine for exploring, but tests should check themselves. This tutorial drives the moving-average filter from Getting started with random data and compares every output against a model written in Julia.","title":"Self-checking testbenches"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testbenches/#A-reference-model","page":"Self-checking testbenches","text":"The filter outputs the average of the four samples taken before the current edge, rounded down:\n\nfunction reference(history)\n    last4 = history[max(end - 4, 1):end-1]       # samples from the previous 4 edges\n    return UInt8(sum(Int, last4; init=0) ÷ 4)\nend","title":"A reference model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testbenches/#Comparing-against-the-design","page":"Self-checking testbenches","text":"Each iteration sets an input, waits for the rising edge that samples it, and checks the output. @testset and @test come from Julia's standard Test library, so this runs unchanged under Pkg.test().\n\nusing VeriJul, Test, Random\n\nsrc = joinpath(pkgdir(VeriJul), \"examples\", \"filter.v\")\ndut = load_dut(src, \"filter\"; build_dir=mktempdir())\nsim = Simulator(dut)\nstart_clock!(sim)\n\n# Flush the history with zeros so the design and the model agree\ndut.in_data = 0\n@tick sim 5\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\nhistory = zeros(UInt8, 4)\n@testset \"filter matches reference\" begin\n    for _ in 1:200\n        x = rand(UInt8)\n        dut.in_data = x\n        @tick sim\n        push!(history, x)\n        @test dut.out_data == reference(history)\n    end\nend\nTest.DefaultTestSet(\"filter matches reference\", false, true, false, \"testbenches.md\", 1.794811078323582e9, Random.Xoshiro(0xfff0241072ddab67, 0xc53bc12f4c3f0b4e, 0x56d451780b2dd4ba, 0x50a4aa153d208dd8, 0x3649a58b3b63d5db), 200, 1.794811078346948e9, 0x01, ReentrantLock(), Any[])","title":"Comparing against the design"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testbenches/#Testbenches-as-tasks","page":"Self-checking testbenches","text":"@testbench defines a function and a launch_<name>(sim) that runs it as a task. That's useful when several things should happen in parallel, for example a driver and a monitor.\n\nThe clock waits for every launched testbench: no edge happens while any of them is busy between two ticks, so both see every edge even if one of them prints or sleeps. A testbench that has finished no longer holds the clock back. Tasks started with plain @async aren't waited for, so launch concurrent testbenches with @testbench.\n\n@testbench function driver(sim)\n    for x in (0x40, 0x40, 0x40, 0x40, 0x00)\n        sim.dut.in_data = x\n        @tick sim\n    end\nend\n\n@testbench function monitor(sim)\n    seen = UInt8[]\n    for _ in 1:8\n        @tick sim\n        push!(seen, sim.dut.out_data)\n    end\n    return seen\nend\n\nd = launch_driver(sim)\nm = launch_monitor(sim)\nwait(d)\nfetch(m)\n8-element Vector{UInt8}:\n 0x5a\n 0x4e\n 0x4e\n 0x47\n 0x40\n 0x30\n 0x20\n 0x10\n\nfetch returns the monitor's result once it finishes. If a task throws, the error is rethrown by wait/fetch, wrapped in a TestbenchError that says when it happened, so failures are never silently lost. Extra arguments to a launcher are passed on: launch_driver(sim, data) runs driver(sim, data). Testing with Test.jl shows how @test works inside testbenches.","title":"Testbenches as tasks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testbenches/#Don't-hang:-timeouts-and-stopped-clocks","page":"Self-checking testbenches","text":"A testbench waiting for an edge that never comes would hang forever. Two things prevent that:\n\ntimeout (wall-clock seconds) on tick or @tick throws a TickTimeout.\nmax_cycles on @wait_until and wait_edge throws a WaitTimeout when the design doesn't respond in time (see Waiting for events).\nstop_clock! wakes every waiting task with a ClockStopped error, and tick throws ClockStopped if the clock isn't running.\n\ntry\n    @tick sim 10^9 timeout=0.5      # far more cycles than 0.5 s allows\ncatch err\n    err\nend\nTickTimeout(0.5)\n\nstop_clock!(sim)\ntry\n    @tick sim\ncatch err\n    err\nend\nClockStopped(\"the simulator clock is not running; call start_clock!(sim)\")\n\n","title":"Don't hang: timeouts and stopped clocks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/testbenches/#Resetting-a-design","page":"Self-checking testbenches","text":"Designs with a reset input can use reset!, which holds the reset for a number of rising edges and then releases it:\n\nreset!(sim)                                  # port :rst, active high, 2 cycles\nreset!(sim, :rst_n; active_high=false, cycles=4)\n\nThe clock must be running. The next tutorial uses it.\n\nNext: Waiting for events.","title":"Resetting a design"}]
}
